body:
AMENDMENT to the approved description 210ab5a0 (it stays as written; this adds scope for the owner to review).

ADDED SCOPE
1. OVERDRIVE overrides. brain/behavior.json "overdrive.overrides" is a deep partial of the finishing, stall, reminders, clarify, context, tools and evidence sections, merged over the base values while OVERDRIVE is on (root sessions only). OVERDRIVE can therefore push harder or softer than normal mode from brain alone. Shipped: { "finishing": { "selfVerify": { "maxRounds": 1 } } }, with base selfVerify.maxRounds 0, which reproduces today's "self-verify only in OVERDRIVE". Subagent children never self-verify.
2. Fuses become bounded counters. selfVerify.maxRounds, incompleteTasks.maxNudges, runtimeEvidence.maxNudges and browserEvidence.maxNudges are 0..3. They ship at today's values (0 or 1), and 0 switches the rung off.
3. OVERDRIVE guards are tighten-only. overdrive.guards.{deletions, protectedDeletions, protectedEdits, outsideWorkspaceEdits} take "run" | "refuse" and all ship as "run".
   - protectedEdits and outsideWorkspaceEdits cover Write and Edit only; a shell redirect is not covered (documented).
   - An explicit user allow rule or literal grant passes a non-protected refusal.
   - The protected decision is taken first.
   - The kill-by-name refusal stays a fixed floor.
   - Three NEW refusal prompts in brain (overdrive deletion, protected edit, outside-workspace edit) are sent only when a guard is set to "refuse".
4. More instructional texts move to brain/prompts with byte-identical rendering:
   - the deletion-policy system section;
   - the permission decline and refusal texts;
   - the two reuse-check reminders;
   - five tool texts: Bash foreground-sleep, the two Read image notes, Write's replaced note, WebSearch disabled.
   The Monitor noise-stop keeps its pre-existing literal {{noiseLimit}} bytes.
5. Brain-level structure.
   - Core system sections carry an "order:" frontmatter, so brain decides which core sections exist and their order.
   - Any prompt file may carry "enabled: false", which switches it off exactly like a blank override.
6. Prose that states a knob's value: the compiler prints a WARNING (not a failure) when a knob moves away from a value stated in prose, naming the prompt to reword.

DELIBERATELY NOT KNOBS (recorded in brain/README.md)
- the ladder order and its bounds mechanism;
- the stall detector's existence;
- deny rules;
- tool_use/tool_result pairing;
- the attended guards;
- the deletion and kill detection lists;
- the self-verify symptom and hedge regexes (ReDoS risk);
- compaction sizing internals;
- cron, wakeup and tool limits stated in pinned descriptions;
- the OVERDRIVE boot stance;
- subagent internals;
- frontend copy describing OVERDRIVE (app/, tui/ — recorded as a known limit).

EXTRA TEST CHECKLIST
7. A knob changed in OVERDRIVE overrides applies only while OVERDRIVE is on.
8. With every guard set to "refuse", OVERDRIVE never asks and never runs the refused call; an explicit allow rule still passes.
9. Reordering or removing a core section file changes the assembled system prompt accordingly; the shipped order reproduces the pinned prompt.
10. enabled: false on a prompt behaves exactly like a blank override.
11. An off-default length-cutoff setting does not stop the context-overflow resume.
